[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
Inter-VLAN reachability from Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) to the 172.16.10.0/24 segment is healthy. Direct evidence:
- Laptop0 successfully pings multiple 172.16.10.x hosts, including PC4 (172.16.10.4) and PC5 (172.16.10.5), with 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT.
- Return path is fine: hosts in 172.16.10.0/24 (PC4/PC5/laptops) can ping Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) successfully.

What likely caused the confusion: Laptop0 cannot ping the VLAN gateway IPs (172.16.20.1 and 172.16.10.1). We confirmed 100% loss to those two addresses from Laptop0, while Router0’s subinterfaces eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) and eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) are up. Router0 has no filtering rules (iptables default ACCEPT) and is forwarding transit traffic between subnets, as proven by end-host reachability. The router does respond to ICMP on its base interface (192.168.1.1), further showing the box is alive, but it is not answering ICMP to the VLAN-interface IPs. This behavior affected only pinging the gateway itself, not inter-subnet forwarding.

Localization: Router0, subinterfaces eth0.10 and eth0.20 (ICMP echo to SVI IPs not answered), but L3 forwarding is operating correctly.

Root cause: Misinterpretation — the router’s VLAN interface IPs are not replying to ICMP, leading to the perception of a block, while actual host-to-host connectivity is normal.

Corrective fix: None required for access to 172.16.10.x. If desired, enable ICMP replies on the SVI IPs (verify OS/ICMP handling on Router0), but user access to PC4 and other 172.16.10.x hosts already works. === END ===